# G0: global coder parameters -- counter rates, mixer, APM, NLMS adaptation.
#
# Every update rate here is a 16-bit fixed-point MULTIPLIER: step = d*mul/65536,
# replacing the shift parameters the model used to carry.  mul = 65536>>shift
# reproduces any old shift EXACTLY (the product is exact and the >>16 is the
# same arithmetic shift), so this file as seeded is byte-identical to the
# hand-tuned shift model -- what it adds is the space between the powers of two,
# which is where the shift schedule could never reach.
#
# Patterns are the optimizer's search space: each bit is one knob opt.pl can
# flip.  Widening a pattern widens the range; the seeded value is what the bits
# spell out now.

Prefix G0
Debug 1
Const 0

# FREEZING GROUPS.  opt.pl's cost is proportional to the total pattern bits it
# can see, and this file is now ~1050 of them.  A '!' at the start of a line
# toggles Debug for that line, so a frozen line emits mdesc instead of pdesc:
# the same object, with a descriptor string that does not carry the "!MAP!"
# marker opt.pl scans for, so the value compiles in unchanged and the knob is
# simply not in the search space.  Freeze what has little leverage on the corpus
# at hand: the block-header (HW*) and literal (LW*) schedules together govern
# under 1% of a typical output, so tuning the code schedule and the predictor
# first is much better spent time.
#
# This did not work until recently.  mdesc/mmask/mmask2 were never defined in
# sh_mapping.inc -- only the p* forms were -- so a '!' line emitted a call to an
# undeclared macro and the build failed, which is why no line here has ever
# carried one.  They are defined now; see IDX-FORMAT.md section 10.

#---------------------------------------------------------------- counters

# Adaptive counter rate schedule, indexed by update count n = 0..15.
# Seeded from the tuned shift schedule {2,2,3,3,4,4,4,5,5,5,5,6,6,6,6,6}
# as 65536>>shift.  Memory of this model says the rate schedule was the single
# biggest knob found by hand (-2.2%), which makes it the first thing to hand to
# opt.pl now that the intermediate rates exist.
Number CW0,  1, 0!1010100101100010
Number CW1,  1, 0!0111110011000001
Number CW2,  1, 0!0101001000000000
Number CW3,  1, 0!0100100100000000
Number CW4,  1, 0!0100010010000011
Number CW5,  1, 0!0011100100100101
Number CW6,  1, 0!0011000000000001
Number CW7,  1, 0!0010011101100000
Number CW8,  1, 0!0010011000001001
Number CW9,  1, 0!0010000000000000
Number CW10, 1, 0!0010000000001000
Number CW11, 1, 0!0001110010000000
Number CW12, 1, 0!0001011110000000
Number CW13, 1, 0!0001011010100001
Number CW14, 1, 0!0001100000000000
Number CW15, 1, 0!0000001000000000

# Rate-index cap.  n indexes CW[0..15] and was capped at 15 in code, i.e. every
# counter eventually reaches the slowest rate in the schedule.  Capping lower
# keeps mature counters more adaptive.
Number CW_NMAX, 1, 0!01111

# Prior every code counter starts from.  Unbiased is CTR_ONE/2; the tree nodes
# below the root are not unbiased in truth, and this is the only handle on that.
Number CP_INIT, 1, 0!1000000000000000

# Slow fixed-rate twin of the primary code counter (was shift 8 -> 65536>>8).
Number SLOW_MUL, 1, 0!0000000100000000
Number SP_INIT,  1, 0!0101110000010000

# Block-header counters (tHnb / tHmn: the magnitude class and mantissa of every
# coded header field).  These see a handful of values per block, not one per
# sample, so the schedule that suits the code tree is not obviously right here.
# Cloned from it, so seeded identical.
Number HW0,   1, 0!0101010000000000
Number HW1,   1, 0!0011010000001000
Number HW2,   1, 0!0100001101000010
Number HW3,   1, 0!0011000000000000
Number HW4,   1, 0!0011000000000100
Number HW5,   1, 0!0010110000010000
Number HW6,   1, 0!0001100000000000
Number HW7,   1, 0!0001000010000000
Number HW8,   1, 0!0010010000001000
Number HW9,   1, 0!0010101000101110
Number HW10,  1, 0!0001011000011001
Number HW11,  1, 0!0010000000000000
Number HW12,  1, 0!0000110000110001
Number HW13,  1, 0!0001011000000001
Number HW14,  1, 0!0001001001000000
Number HW15,  1, 0!0000010000000000
Number HW_NMAX, 1, 0!01100
Number HP_INIT, 1, 0!0101000000000000

# Literal-byte counters (tLit, and the IMA reserved-byte flag).  Stored RIFF
# headers, trailing chunks, runt blocks and the MS coefficient-set byte -- all
# near-static data where a fast early rate mostly costs noise.  Cloned from the
# code schedule, so seeded identical.
Number LW0,   1, 0!1011111100000010
Number LW1,   1, 0!0111100000000000
Number LW2,   1, 0!0100110000000001
Number LW3,   1, 0!0010000000000000
Number LW4,   1, 0!0011000100100010
Number LW5,   1, 0!0011100000000000
Number LW6,   1, 0!0011000000000000
Number LW7,   1, 0!0001110000000101
Number LW8,   1, 0!0000100000000000
Number LW9,   1, 0!0010100000000000
Number LW10,  1, 0!0010100000000100
Number LW11,  1, 0!0000000001000010
Number LW12,  1, 0!0011000000000000
Number LW13,  1, 0!0000010000000000
Number LW14,  1, 0!0000010000000000
Number LW15,  1, 0!0000111000100000
Number LW_NMAX, 1, 0!01111
Number LP_INIT, 1, 0!0101001100100000

#---------------------------------------------------------------- mixer

# Learning rate: dw = st*err*MLR >> 20.  Seeded 256 = 2^(20-12), i.e. the old
# >>12.
Number MLR, 1, 0!0000000001100101

# Weight init and clamp (clamp as log2, so 16 = the old +-65536).
Number W_INIT,   1, 0!0000101100011000
Number W_CLAMPL, 1, 1!11011

# Bias input, in the stretched domain.
Number BIAS, 1, 0!0000000000

#---------------------------------------------------------------- APM

# Blend numerators: pf = (AB*pa + (4-AB)*p) >> 2, one per stage.
Number AB1, 1, 0!01
Number AB2, 1, 0!01

# Structure and rate, one pair per stage instance:
#   AP_N_* = interpolation interval count (cells = N+1; 32 -> the old 33 cells).
#            The fraction is 12-bit regardless of N, so N is free of the old
#            power-of-two constraint.
#   AP_R_* = update rate multiplier: t += (g-t)*frac*R >> 28.  1024 reproduces
#            the old >>13 exactly at N = 32.
Number AP_N_A, 1, 0!00000100001
Number AP_R_A, 1, 0!0000000101000010
Number AP_N_B, 1, 0!00000011100
Number AP_R_B, 1, 0!0000001110101100

#---------------------------------------------------------------- signal predictor

# NLMS stage lengths.  Storage is sized for PRED_NMAX = 512 taps, so these are
# free to move; mixing a short and a long horizon beat any single length by
# hand, but the three were never fitted jointly.  Base 1 with 9 bits gives the
# optimizer [1, 512] each.
Number N1, 1, 1!110000011
Number N2, 1, 1!111111111
Number N3, 1, 1!100111101

# Cross-channel stage: taps on the OTHER channel's recent deltas, adapted on
# what the three own-signal stages leave over.  0 disables it, which is what
# mono does anyway (the taps are all zero, so nothing moves and the output is
# bit-identical either way).  Base 0 with 9 bits gives [0, 511].
#
# Unlike the own-signal stages this one wants to be SHORT: 8 taps beat 4, 16, 32
# and 64 on every stereo file measured, and 32+ was a net loss on material with
# weakly related channels.  MU4 wants to be gentle for the same reason -- 8 beat
# 4 by 0.5%, and both 6 and 10 were worse.  EFL4 made no measurable difference,
# so it is seeded neutral and left for the optimizer.
#
# XPRED: how deep a forecast the channel coded FIRST at an instant hands to the
# others.  Its real delta is a whole sample away, but its own stages already
# have an opinion about it, and that opinion is same-instant.  0 = do not
# forecast (use the previous instant's real delta), 1..3 = sum that many stages.
#
# Measured NEGATIVE on every file (+2824 B on the stereo music case at depth 1,
# worse still at 2 and 3), and on reflection that is the right answer rather
# than a tuning miss: the cross taps ALREADY hold the other channel's recent
# deltas, so any linear forecast the other channel's stages could make is a
# linear function of taps this filter can see.  Handing it the forecast trades
# real information for a smoothed version of the same thing.  Kept as a knob at
# 0 -- it costs nothing off, and the reasoning only holds while the cross stage
# stays linear.
Number XPRED, 1, 0!00
Number N4, 1, 0!000100001

# Stage-1 tap 0 at reset: 1<<WSH makes the untrained predictor a linear
# extrapolation of the last two samples, which is what the first samples of the
# first block get coded against.
Number PW0, 1, 0!10101010000011000

# NLMS per-stage adaptation gain (the shift added to the energy scale).
Number MU1, 1, 0!0111
Number MU2, 1, 0!0101
Number MU3, 1, 0!0100
Number MU4, 1, 0!1000

# NLMS per-stage energy floor multiplier: the normaliser is en + n*EFL, so 1 is
# the current "one unit per tap".  Raising it damps adaptation on quiet passages
# without touching the loud-signal behaviour, which no single MU can do.
Number EFL1, 1, 1!0100
Number EFL2, 1, 1!0010
Number EFL3, 1, 1!1101
Number EFL4, 1, 1!1101

#---------------------------------------------------------------- match model

# Window lengths of the two models, in codes.  The short one earns its place as
# a high-order context; the long one as a repeat detector.  Measured separately:
# the short optimum is 5 (order 4 and 6 are both worse on ordinary audio) and
# the long one flattens out from 12 up.  HB/TB are log2 of the shared history
# ring and of each model's hash table.
Number MATCH_ORDER, 1, 0!000111
Number MATCH_ORDER2, 1, 0!010010
Number MATCH_HB,    1, 0!10110
Number MATCH_TB,    1, 0!11110

# ...and the long model's own, seeded identical to the short one's so the
# archive does not move.  They were one shared pair until now, which forced a
# 5-code window and a 63-code window to use the same ring and the same hash
# table even though they do different jobs -- the short model is a high-order
# context that fires on nearly every symbol, the long one a repeat detector
# that fires rarely and wants reach rather than density.  Nothing about the
# storage was shared (each MatchModel has always owned its vectors), only the
# two numbers, so separating them costs nothing and gives opt.pl the knob.
Number MATCH_HB2,   1, 0!00111
Number MATCH_TB2,   1, 0!11110

# Feed match-present into the mixer's weight-set selection as well as into the
# counters (1), or leave the selection alone (0).  It doubles the weight table.
Number MATCH_MIXSEL, 1, 0!1

# Match counters (tM).  A match of any length is either near-certain or plain
# wrong, which is a different regime from the ordinary code contexts, so the
# schedule is its own rather than shared.  Cloned from the code schedule.
Number MW0,   1, 0!0100000000000000
Number MW1,   1, 0!0100000000000000
Number MW2,   1, 0!0010000000000000
Number MW3,   1, 0!0010000000000000
Number MW4,   1, 0!0001000000000000
Number MW5,   1, 0!0001000000000000
Number MW6,   1, 0!0001000000000000
Number MW7,   1, 0!0000100000000000
Number MW8,   1, 0!0000100000000000
Number MW9,   1, 0!0000100000000000
Number MW10,  1, 0!0000100000000000
Number MW11,  1, 0!0000010000000000
Number MW12,  1, 0!0000010000000000
Number MW13,  1, 0!0000010000000000
Number MW14,  1, 0!0000010000000000
Number MW15,  1, 0!0000010000000000
Number MW_NMAX, 1, 0!01111
Number MP_INIT, 1, 0!1111000000010000

#---------------------------------------------------------------- solid mode

# At a wav boundary in a solid stream: keep the NLMS weights as a warm start
# (1) or start the filters from scratch (0).  The sample histories are cleared
# either way.  Which wins depends on whether consecutive files are the same
# kind of material -- it is a corpus property, so it is a knob, not a constant.
Number SEG_KEEPW, 1, 0!1

#---------------------------------------------------------------- context widths

# Magnitude classes that get their own mantissa rows in code_signed; classes
# above this share the last row.
Number SGN_NCLS, 1, 0!000000

# Confidence buckets: where inside its quantizer cell the prediction sat.
Number NCONF, 1, 1!001

# Decay of the two MS iDelta-predictor cost trackers (as a shift).
#---------------------------------------------------------------- second cascade
# Shape of the parallel NLMS cascade.  Seeded from the other optimizer run's
# optimum, because that is the point: the two runs disagree about the right
# horizon and both are right on some material, so both shapes run and the mixer
# picks per file.  These are ordinary tunables -- opt.pl can move them, and
# setting them equal to the primary set makes the second cascade redundant
# rather than harmful (it just costs its mixer inputs).
Number N1B,    1, 1!000000111
Number N2B,    1, 1!000000011
Number N3B,    1, 1!000000001
Number N4B,    1, 0!000110000
Number MU1B,   1, 0!1000
Number MU2B,   1, 0!1001
Number MU3B,   1, 0!1000
Number MU4B,   1, 0!0100
Number EFL1B,  1, 1!1010
Number EFL2B,  1, 1!1001
Number EFL3B,  1, 1!0010
Number EFL4B,  1, 1!1101
Number PW0B,   1, 0!10010000100001101

#---------------------------------------------------------------- coder shape
# These were literals or #defines inside xadpcm.cpp.  They are here because
# each one is safe for opt.pl to sweep: any value in range produces a working
# codec, only a better or worse one.  The numbers that are NOT safe -- P_BITS,
# CTR_BITS, MIX_NIN, LIT_W, PRED_NMAX and the rest of the arithmetic contract --
# stay in the .cpp, because changing them does not tune the model, it breaks it.

# Stretch units per nat: horizontal scale of the squash/stretch tables, and the
# unit -v reports in.  Decides how much of the logistic domain the mixer's
# fixed-point weights can reach.
Number ST_SCALE, 1, 0!0100000000

# Weight fixed-point scale for the NLMS taps: fractional bits per tap.  Too few
# and small corrections quantize away, too many and the products lose headroom.
Number WSH, 1, 8!1110

# Clamp on the summed prediction, and the guard on the adaptation shift.
Number PRED_DMAX,  1, 0!00101000000101001
Number PRED_SHMAX, 1, 0!010000

# Widest magnitude class the header-residual tree may name.  NOT a tuning knob:
# above the cap a value is coded as the cap, and the encoder then goes on using
# the value it actually had, so the decoder ends up somewhere else.  Header
# residuals DO reach it -- the claim that they never do was the whole bug, and
# a sweep that scored size alone drove this to 0 and made both test files
# undecodable.  17 covers the worst case a caller can present (a 16-bit field
# against a 16-bit-clamped prediction, zigzagged), and nothing above it is ever
# reached, so there is nothing here to search: frozen, and clamped in
# xad_codec.inc as well.
!Number SGN_MAXNB, 1, 17!0000

# Two literal sites have no real previous byte to key on and borrow a synthetic
# one.  Each shares a row with any literal whose predecessor happened to equal
# it -- dilution, not a correctness problem -- so where they sit is open.
Number LIT_CTX_TAILBITS, 1, 0!11001000
Number LIT_CTX_NIBBLE,   1, 0!11011100

Number HJ_BITS, 1, 8!10101
